// An untyped `[]` returned as `List[Int]` and handed to a `List[Str]`
// parameter at once. The frontend admits it, and the go reference REFUSES it
// by name: no one Go type satisfies both flows (issue #2898).
//
// This is the conflicting-targets refusal, not the no-target one: both flows
// are concrete List types, so `_empty_list_type` reaches its two-evidence
// branch and has to reject the document rather than pick whichever flow the
// walk saw first. The refusal also covers the second offer to a binding, which
// is why the `targets` list has to keep every distinct declared type instead
// of only the first.
//
// Not a byte-agreement case, so it cannot live in the tier's CORPUS.
fn takes(xs: List[Str]) -> Bool { return xs.length() == 0 }
fn c() -> List[Int] {
  var out = []
  let e = takes(out)
  return out
}
